course: dictionary
lesson: 11619
title: Approved: officially allowed
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Approved means officially accepted or allowed. It can describe a thing, like an approved helmet, or show that someone said yes, like when Mum approved a plan. Approve is the verb, and approved is its past form.
explain: «Only wear an approved helmet when you cycle.» Here approved describes the helmet: people in charge have checked it and said it is safe to use. «Mum approved my plan to visit the park.» Here approved is a verb: Mum said yes.
explain: Look for clues in the words around it. Approved by, government-approved and approved of are common phrases. Approved of means you liked or agreed with someone or something.
fact: Approved means officially accepted or allowed.
fact: «Approved» is the past form of the verb approve, so «Mum approved my plan» happened in the past.
fact: «Approved» can also describe a thing: an approved list is a list that people in charge have accepted.
fact: «Approved by» means someone in charge has said yes, as in «a trip approved by the head».
fact: «Approved of» means you liked or agreed with someone or something.
exercise: «Only wear an approved helmet when you cycle.» What does approved mean here? | *Officially accepted as safe or allowed | Painted in bright colours | Lost or missing | Broken and old
exercise: «Mum approved my plan to visit the park.» What does approved mean? | Forgot all about it | Wrote a long letter | *Said yes and gave permission | Said no and stopped it
exercise: «The trip was approved by the head teacher.» Find and copy the one word that means officially allowed. | trip | *approved | school | teacher
test: «The teacher approved of our idea for the science fair.» What does approved of mean? | Hated it and said no | *Liked and agreed with it | Lost the idea | Copied it onto a poster
test: «The website is on the school's approved list.» What is an approved list? | *A list that people in charge have accepted | A list someone wrote in the dark | A shopping list | A list that is always wrong
exam: «The bridge was government-approved, so it was safe to cross.» What does government-approved suggest? | Built by children for fun | Closed for ever | Painted red by mistake | *Officially accepted by the government as safe
